## 1. THE ARCHITECTURE: THREE DECORRELATED SLEEVES

The core insight (from Round 11 Contestant F, mathematically verified): a single trading edge cannot produce 10-15% monthly returns within a 7-8% drawdown budget. The expected maximum drawdown for one edge over 1,000 trades is approximately 15R, which at 0.50% risk already equals 7.5% -- your entire budget on a single edge.

The solution is genuine diversification across three sleeves that profit in different market regimes. Returns add linearly; risk adds as the square root (sub-linear). This is the mathematical basis for real risk reduction.

| Sleeve | Market Regime | Session (UK Time) | Instruments | Edge |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| A: Session-Open Sweep | Normal volatility | London 07:00-11:00, NY 13:30-16:00 | EURUSD, GBPUSD, USDJPY, XAUUSD | Liquidity sweep and reclaim at session opens |
| B: Volatility-Expansion Continuation | Trending / high volatility | London 07:00-15:30, NY 13:30-19:30 | XAUUSD, DAX (GER40), US30 | Breakout that holds, entered on first pullback |
| C: Asian Mean Reversion | Dead / low volatility | 00:00-06:30 | AUDNZD, EURGBP, EURCHF | Fade 2-standard-deviation Bollinger touches back to mean |

**Why this works:** Sleeve A profits when volatility is normal. Sleeve B profits when volatility is expanding and markets are trending -- exactly the days when Sleeve A gets chopped and stopped out. Sleeve C profits when volatility is dead and the other two have no setups. Their bad days rarely overlap by construction.

**Correlation enforcement:** Measure daily P&L correlation between sleeves on a rolling 60-day window. If any two sleeves drift above 0.50 correlation, halve the smaller one. Target correlation is 0.30 or below. At 0.30 correlation, the risk multiplier is 2.19x across three sleeves, which allows 0.24% risk per trade while staying inside the 8% drawdown budget.

