## 7. THE 10-YEAR SURVIVAL ARCHITECTURE

### 7.1 Capital Structure

- 3 x $50,000 prop accounts at three different prop firms
- Trade-copied across all three
- 0.08% risk per trade per account (0.24% combined across three accounts)
- Never more than 40% of total capital at any one firm
- Keep 12 months of challenge fees in reserve as a rebuild fund

### 7.2 Monthly Profit Withdrawal (The Most Important Rule)

- Fix a compounding base (target: $150,000 across all accounts)
- Every month, withdraw 70% of all profit above the base
- Compound the remaining 30% back into the base quarterly
- At 10% monthly on a $150,000 base: $15,000/month, $10,500 withdrawn, $4,500 compounded
- Over 10 years at this rate: approximately $1.26 million in withdrawn cash, with the base growing to approximately $650,000
- Cash in your bank is the only profit that a broker, a gap, or your own future mistake can never take back

### 7.3 Edge-Decay Monitor

This is the module that most 10-year attempts lack, and the reason most die in year 3.

- Track rolling 30-trade expectancy per instrument x per session, for every sleeve
- If any instrument-session combination drops below 0.10R expectancy: that combination auto-pauses and alerts. It does not resume until manually reviewed and re-validated
- Every January: run a full walk-forward re-validation on the most recent 12 months of data
- Maximum 4 tunable parameters per sleeve, ever. A parameter that only works at one exact value is curve-fitting. Delete it
- Plan to retire and replace one sleeve every 2-3 years. The sweep-reclaim edge has been degrading as algorithmic execution gets faster. The structure survives; the instruments and parameters evolve
- Budget one week per quarter to research a replacement sleeve

### 7.4 Shadow Account

- Run the same algorithm on a demo account at fixed risk with no governors (no throttle, no kill switches)
- When live and shadow diverge, the difference is execution, not edge
- This diagnostic has saved more accounts than any indicator

### 7.5 Infrastructure Requirements

- VPS in the broker's datacentre (LD4 for most FX and prop feeds), ping below 5ms
- Primary and backup VPS in the broker's region
- Economic calendar feed with automatic Tier-1 news blackout, plus a manual override file
- Tick-level backtesting only (Dukascopy or broker tick data) with real variable spread. M1 OHLC backtests will overstate this strategy by 30-50%
- Trade log per entry: score breakdown, spread at entry, latency, requested vs filled price, MFE (maximum favorable excursion), MAE (maximum adverse excursion), exit reason. Without slippage logging you cannot diagnose live underperformance
- Broker-side emergency stop-losses (not just client-side)
- Separate watchdog process that flattens everything if the main process loses connection
- Automatic position reconciliation with the broker every 60 seconds
- Trading disabled when market data becomes stale (no tick for 10+ seconds)
- Daily database and configuration backups
- Alerts for: spread spikes, latency, rejected orders, equity limits

### 7.6 Prop-Firm Compliance

Many prop firms have a consistency rule: the biggest single day cannot exceed 20-45% of total profit. With approximately 200 trades per month at 0.24% risk, the strategy naturally complies. A single 3% day would breach it at some firms. Monitor this per firm.

